  "query_preview": "..The monsoon ridge axis, currently orientated NE \nto SW from Colorado to nrn Baja will become E-W orientated over \nthe next 48 hours with the high centered W of nrn Baja. Well\nabove normal high temperatures with moderate to major HeatRisk\nfor much of the area. Extreme Heat Warning in effect today thru\nWednesday evening for the much of Pima county, south-central and\nsoutheast Pinal county and the Gila River vly of Graham county.\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery showed scattered debris clouds.\nSatellite derived PWAT values ranged from 0.90\" to 1.15\" while \nat the surface dew points were mostly in the 40s to lower 50s.\nConvective activity today will be a bit more in areal coverage\nwith western extent over eastern Pima and Santa Cruz counties\nwhere 700 mb theta-e ridge will reside. Main threat from storms\nthis afternoon/early evening will be outflow winds of 35 to 45 \nmph with low end chance of gusts over 50 mph, blowing dust and \nbrief moderate rain with strongest cells. Steering flow will \nmove storms from generally the NE to the SW. Wednesday looks \nkinda similar to today. \n\nThursday and Friday: The centered of the monsoon high that will\nbe west of nrn Baja on Wednesday lifts NE across the state\nduring this period. Some minor drying of PWATs across the area \nwhich may limit thunderstorm activity to south and east of \nTucson both days. Highs remaining on the hot side.\n\nThis weekend: As the monsoon high moves into the central Rockies\nthe flow aloft becomes more favorable out of the E-SE. PWATs,\nper ensembles, increase areawide thanks to moisture influx from\nSonoran thunderstorm outflows moving north and potential gulf \nsurges. The added moisture this will increase the flash flooding\nthreat with Saturday, at this time, likely having the highest \nthreat. Highs will be around normal.",
